{"study_accession":"PRJNA1328907","secondary_study_accession":"SRP620583","title":"Optimized CRISPR Perturbation Illuminates the Functions of RNA Modification Proteins","description":"we have previously developed an in situ CRISPR screening platform in mice named iMAP, where Cas9 guides are stored in germline-transmitted transgenes but inducibly released (expressed) via Cre-mediated recombination. A major limitation of iMAP is a strong bias in guide representation following the recombination. We now report that this bias was effectively mitigated by eliminating a recombination hotspot at the transgene. We next profiled, across 46 tissues, the abundance of the guides targeting 71 (52%) genes encoding writers, readers and erasers of RNA modifications, revealing their context-specific functions. Furthermore, we identified, using a mouse tumor model, several candidate regulators of mouse NK cell activation, and found that their ortholog knockout boosted human NK cells, thus offering potential targets for improving cancer immunotherapy. Finally, a public database was established for accessing and analyzing iMAP data. Our study marks an important step toward the creation of the Perturbation Atlas.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":3,"study_labels":[],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN61618871","study_accession":"PRJNA1328907","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","description":"KO","sex":"not applicable","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2026-07-16","attributes":[["strain","not applicable"],["isolate","basal-KO-2"],["breed","not applicable"],["cultivar","not applicable"],["ecotype","not applicable"],["age","not applicable"],["dev_stage","not applicable"],["collection_date","not applicable"],["geo_loc_name","not applicable"],["sex","not applicable"],["tissue","PBMC"],["biomaterial_provider","missing"],["treatment","basal"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR39612961","experiment_accession